# The product of balls with one-limb centers, with the special states of a ball:
# ball_mul_1, then the fix-up of milestone1.md, "Ball kernels".
# finite_when (statement B4): the result is a finite ball when no product x y of members of the
# operands exceeds a sixteenth of the overflow threshold in magnitude,
# (|cx| + rx) (|cy| + ry) <= 2^(emax - 4). A sixteenth and not a quarter because the statement holds
# from two bits of precision, where the constants of the radius are large (G = 4).
kernel:        ball_mul_1_full
input:         stored cx
input:         stored rx
input:         stored cy
input:         stored ry
output:        cw
output:        rw
value:         cx*cy
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       encloses cw rw x*y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry finite_when=abs_le((abs(cx)+rx)*(abs(cy)+ry),-4)
min_precision: 2
source:        ball_mul_1, then the fix-up of the special states of a ball (Milestone 1 listing)
license:       core: as ball_mul_1; fix-up: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeBallFinite.v ball_mul_1_full_correct
entry:         yes
ieee_only:     yes
cone_exempt:   the fix-up selects, after explicit tests that the center and the radius are finite (f) and that no operand is empty (e); B1 to B4 of milestone1.md, "Ball kernels", state what comes out

(cz, rz) = ball_mul_1(cx, rx, cy, ry)
q        = add(rx, ry)
e        = eq(q, q)
a        = abs(cz)
f1       = lt(a, const inf)
f2       = lt(rz, const inf)
f        = and(f1, f2)
c0       = select(f, cz, 0)
r        = select(f, rz, const inf)
cw       = select(e, c0, q)
rw       = select(e, r, q)
